# Test-only environment. COMMITTED ON PURPOSE — fixture data, no secrets.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# DEFAULT_CONFIG in src/main.ts is compiled in from `import.meta.env.VITE_*`,
# which vite.config.ts resolves as `env.VITE_API_URL || 'https://api.vizora.io'`.
# The `update_config` allowlist (F43) is anchored to those compiled-in defaults
# and accepts a candidate only when its registrable domain matches, so the whole
# allowlist test suite depends on what these resolve to.
#
# That made the suite depend on a developer's gitignored `.env`. With no `.env`
# the fallbacks apply and everything passes — which is why CI was always green.
# With a real `.env` pointing at a different domain (e.g. vizora.cloud), the
# compiled-in default changes underneath the tests and five allowlist tests fail
# on a completely clean checkout. The failures looked like a Node-version problem
# because CI and local machines differed in Node too; they were not.
#
# Vite loads `.env.[mode]` after `.env`, and vitest runs with mode=test, so these
# values win over any local `.env` for tests only. Local dev and every build are
# unaffected.
#
# Keep these aligned with the fallbacks in vite.config.ts `define`. The domain
# matters: the allowlist specs push `*.vizora.io` hosts and assert they are
# accepted as same-registrable-domain.
VITE_API_URL=https://api.vizora.io
VITE_REALTIME_URL=wss://realtime.vizora.io
VITE_DASHBOARD_URL=https://dashboard.vizora.io
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=
